<% content_for :title, "A reminder app for elderly parents — free, and spoken out loud" %>
<%#
  Written for the search, not for us. Someone typing "reminder app for elderly
  parents" is asking whether such a thing exists for a parent who will not use a
  phone app, so the first clause of the description answers that and the rest
  says what it costs.

  Held to the same standard as the homepage on the one claim that matters:
  Remindly knows a reminder was *marked done* on the device. It cannot know
  whether a tablet was swallowed, and medication is the wrong subject to be
  loose about in the sentence a search engine prints.

  The telephone belongs in this sentence rather than further down the page. The
  search this page answers is asked by people whose parent will not use an app,
  and for some of them there is no tablet in the house at all — the old wording,
  "nothing to install, no phone to check", told exactly those readers they were
  in the wrong place.
%>
<% content_for :description, "A free reminder app for elderly parents that speaks each reminder out loud — on a tablet or computer they already own, or as a telephone call if there is no screen in the house. You set the reminders from wherever you are, and for medication you are emailed when one is marked done, or when the time passes and nobody marks it." %>

<%#
  Three questions, and none of them the questions /faq answers.

  The first version of this page asked five that were reworded /faq entries —
  what device, what if they are not good with technology, can I set it up from
  away, will I know if one is missed, what does it cost — and published them as
  a second FAQPage. Rewording does not separate two pages: the intent is what
  competes, so both URLs were bidding for the same informational searches with
  our own content on each side.

  These three are the questions someone asks when deciding *whether to bother*,
  which is the state this page's visitor is actually in. /faq answers what
  Remindly is; this answers whether it is worth setting up. There is no FAQPage
  graph here either — /faq keeps that entity, so there is nothing for a crawler
  to weigh one against the other.
%>
<% questions = [
     [
       "Is it worth it if my parent only takes one tablet a day?",
       "That depends on whether the one is ever forgotten. If it never is, this solves a problem you do not have, and you should not bother. Most families arrive here because something has already been missed — and once it is set up, the same routine carries the things that are not medication at all: the appointment on Thursday, a glass of water in the afternoon, the walk after lunch."
     ],
     [
       "Will my parent find it patronising?",
       "That is the objection worth taking seriously, and the reason the page is quiet by default: it says nothing at all unless something is due, keeps no score, and does not nag. There is no streak to break and no report card. What comes back to you is whether a reminder was marked done — not where they are, not what they are doing, and nothing the microphone hears."
     ],
     [
       "What if we set it up and it turns out not to suit us?",
       "Then you stop, and there is nothing to cancel — no card was entered and no subscription started. Your parent can end a caregiver's access themselves at any time, from their own screen, without asking anyone; it is their arrangement to leave as much as yours. Ask and the data is deleted."
     ]
   ] %>

<h1>A reminder app for elderly parents</h1>

<p class="lead">
  The difficulty is rarely that a parent refuses to take their tablets. It is
  that eleven o'clock arrives while they are doing something else, and there is
  nobody in the room to say so. Remindly says it out loud — on a tablet or
  computer they already own, at the time it is due, without their having to
  check a phone or open an app. If there is no tablet in the house, and no
  appetite for one, it telephones them and says the same thing.
</p>

<%#
  Conditional, like the one at the foot of the page. This route is indexable and
  a signed-in reader can arrive on it from a bookmark or a search result, and
  SessionsController#new does not bounce an authenticated visitor — so a fixed
  link to /login put the page's main action on an email form they have no use
  for.
%>
