<p class="free-note">
  <strong>Free — no card, no ads, no sales calls.</strong> No trial that quietly
  ends, and no limit on how many reminders you set.
</p>

<%#
  The criteria, before the product. Someone at this search has usually looked at
  two or three things already and cannot tell what separates them, and the
  useful thing to hand them is the list of questions to ask — which stays useful
  whether or not they choose Remindly. No named comparisons: the questions are
  the point, and a page that spends its space on other people's shortcomings is
  a worse answer to the search than one that answers it.
%>
<h2>What to look for in one</h2>

<p>
  Most reminder software is written for the person who owns the phone. That is a
  different problem from this one — here, the person who needs reminding and the
  person doing the arranging are not the same person, and are often not in the
  same house. Four things follow from that, and they are worth checking against
  anything you are considering:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>Does the reminder reach them without their doing anything?</strong>
    A notification on a phone in another room is not a reminder. Spoken out
    loud, in the room they are in, is — from a tablet if there is one, and
    down the telephone if there is not.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Can you change it from where you are?</strong> A dose time moves
    after an appointment. If that means a visit, or talking someone through a
    settings screen over the telephone, it will not get done.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Does anything come back to you?</strong> Setting a reminder and
    hearing nothing afterwards leaves you exactly where you started — ringing to
    ask.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>What does it ask of them?</strong> Every password, unlock and menu
    is somewhere it can stop working on a day nobody is there to fix it.
  </li>
</ul>

<section class="setup">
  <h2>How Remindly answers them</h2>

  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>You set the reminders</strong> — medication, appointments, a walk
      after lunch — from your own phone or computer, from wherever you happen to
      live.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>They hear it spoken aloud</strong> at the right time, at home,
      without reading a screen or opening anything — from the tablet, or as a
      telephone call that asks them to press one button.
    </li>
    <li>
      <%# "another phone call" until the calls arrived: it meant the caregiver's
          call, but on a page that now says Remindly telephones the care
          receiver, the same words read as a contradiction. %>
      <strong>You see what was marked done</strong>, and what wasn't — so
      checking in does not have to mean ringing to ask.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <p class="setup-foot">
    Their end needs no password: signing in is a link sent by email, and in
    practice whoever sets it up does that once and leaves the page open.
  </p>
</section>

<%#
  Placed after the setup section rather than inside it, because it answers a
  reader who has already decided the setup above will not work in their house.
  The scam objection is met head on: an automated call to an older person looks
  exactly like the calls they have been told to hang up on, and a page that does
  not say so is not being honest with the person it is asking to arrange this.
%>
<section class="setup">
  <h2>If there is no tablet in the house</h2>

  <p>
    Some families never get past the first requirement, because the person being
    cared for does not own a tablet and is not going to start now. Remindly can
    telephone them instead. An ordinary landline is enough — nothing to install,
    no account, no screen — and the reminder is spoken down the line when it is
    due, between 8am and 9pm at their end.
  </p>

  <p>
    The objection worth taking seriously is that an automated call to an elderly
    person looks exactly like the calls they have rightly been taught to put the
    phone down on. So the first call is not a reminder at all. It gives the name
    of the person who arranged the calls, and it says plainly that Remindly will
    never ask for personal details — no bank, no address, no date of birth,
    nothing to read out. A reminder call follows only if they press 1. Press 9
    and there is no second call.
  </p>

  <p>
    After that, each call asks for a single button: <strong>1</strong> for done,
    <strong>2</strong> to be rung again shortly, <strong>9</strong> to stop the
    reminder calls. Nine works on every call, not only the first. You can ask
    them to reconsider later, and that request is itself a call — but asking is
    the whole of what you can do. Only they can start the reminders again, from
    their own keypad.
  </p>

  <p>
    If nobody picks up, Remindly rings again — up to three times, fewer if 9pm
    arrives first. Whether that reaches you depends on the reminder: mark one as
    a reminder where <strong>being late matters</strong> and an unanswered call
    is reported to you straight away. Otherwise you get the ordinary missed
    notice an hour later, and only for the categories you have switched on.
  </p>

  <p class="setup-foot">
    Calls are spoken in English. A number has to be entered and confirmed before
    the first one is made.
  </p>
</section>

<figure class="shot">
  <img src="/screenshot-voice-reminders.webp"
       width="1400" height="1179" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
       alt="The Remindly screen a senior sees: a heading reading My Reminders, a note that there are six reminders today, then each reminder as a large card — Take your morning medication at 8:00 AM with the note two white tablets with breakfast, and Have a glass of water at 10:30 AM — each with a big green Done button and a Snooze button beside it.">
  <figcaption>
    <strong>What your parent sees.</strong> Large type, and only ever two things
    to press.
  </figcaption>
</figure>

<h2>What it runs on</h2>

<p>
  Any device with a web browser and a speaker. Families use an old tablet that
  had stopped being carried around, a laptop that already lives on the desk, or
  the desktop computer in the corner. Nothing is installed and no account is
  needed at the app store, because there is no app to fetch from one.
</p>

<p>
  It works best on a device that already sits somewhere they spend time — the
  kitchen table, beside a favourite chair — with the page left open, the way a
  clock sits on a shelf.
</p>

<div class="note">
  <p>
    <strong>Two things worth knowing before you set this up.</strong>
  </p>
  <p>
    Remindly speaks through the page, so that page has to stay open on their
    device. Close the tab and nothing is spoken.
  </p>
  <p>
    And browsers will not play sound until someone has touched the screen, so on
    an iPad Remindly asks for a single tap after it starts up. It remembers
    anything it could not say and speaks it once you do.
  </p>
</div>

<h2>What comes back to you</h2>

<p>
  You see the whole day at a glance from your own dashboard. For medication,
  Remindly emails you when a reminder is <strong>marked done</strong> on their
  device, and again if the time passes and it has not been. That is the part
  that replaces the phone call.
</p>

<div class="note">
  <p>
    <strong>What "done" means, and what it doesn't.</strong> Remindly records
    that someone pressed <strong>Done</strong> on the reminder. That is a good
    signal and it is not proof: it cannot tell you a tablet was actually
    swallowed, and it is not a substitute for a pill dispenser, a carer or
    medical advice. If a missed dose would be dangerous, Remindly should not be
    the only thing standing between your parent and that dose.
  </p>
</div>

<p>
  Alongside the reminders that repeat, there is a shared list of the one-off
  things — a prescription to collect, an appointment on Thursday. If more than
  one of you is helping, you can each take what you can and see what the others
  have picked up.
</p>

<h2>Before you set it up</h2>

<section class="questions">
